112	<body>
113	<main>
114	  <div class="scoreboard">
115	    <span class="pill">Lesson 0006</span>
116	    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="c1" />§1 Returns</label>
117	    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="c2" />§2 Vol &amp; Sharpe</label>
118	    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="c3" />§3 Drawdown &amp; CAGR</label>
119	    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="c4" />§4 Covariance &amp; CUSUM</label>
120	    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="c5" />§5 In this project</label>
121	    <span class="lbl">Quiz: <strong id="quizScore">0 / 5</strong></span>
122	  </div>
123	
124	  <h1>Statistics Math in Trading</h1>
125	  <p class="lead">
126	    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: <code>analyze_equity.py</code>
127	    is 40 lines that compute every headline number you'll ever quote for a strategy —
128	    CAGR, Sharpe, max drawdown, annualized vol. Each line encodes a statistical idea
129	    you need to defend. This lesson walks the math behind those lines so you can
130	    interpret the numbers honestly and write the next ones yourself.
131	  </p>
132	
133	  <!-- ===================== §1 RETURNS ===================== -->
134	  <h2>1. Returns: simple vs log, and why both exist</h2>
135	
136	  <p>
137	    Every metric in this lesson starts with <strong>returns</strong>, not prices.
138	    (Recall Lesson 0005: returns are roughly stationary; prices aren't.) Two
139	    standard definitions:
140	  </p>
141	
142	  <div class="formula">
143	    r<sub>t</sub><sup>simple</sup> = (P<sub>t</sub> − P<sub>t−1</sub>) / P<sub>t−1</sub>  =  P<sub>t</sub>/P<sub>t−1</sub> − 1
144	    <span class="small-note">Simple return — what <code>pct_change()</code> computes</span>
145	  </div>
146	
147	  <div class="formula">
148	    r<sub>t</sub><sup>log</sup> = ln(P<sub>t</sub> / P<sub>t−1</sub>)
149	    <span class="small-note">Log return — additive across time, used in academic papers and risk math</span>
150	  </div>
151	
152	  <p>The two properties that make each useful:</p>
153	
154	  <table>
155	    <tr><th></th><th>Simple return</th><th>Log return</th></tr>
156	    <tr><td>Compounds via</td><td><code>∏(1 + r)</code> &nbsp; (product)</td><td><code>∑ r</code> &nbsp; (sum)</td></tr>
157	    <tr><td>Aggregates across <em>assets</em> linearly?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (portfolio return = Σ wᵢ rᵢ)</td><td>No</td></tr>
158	    <tr><td>Aggregates across <em>time</em> linearly?</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (multi-day log return = sum of daily log returns)</td></tr>
159	    <tr><td>Symmetric around 0?</td><td>No (−50% / +100% ≠ same magnitude)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
160	  </table>
161	
162	  <p>
163	    Both columns are right, in different contexts. This repo uses <strong>simple
164	    returns</strong> throughout (<code>pct_change()</code> everywhere) because the
165	    main operation is portfolio aggregation: <em>"how did my equally-weighted top-N
166	    do today?"</em> Portfolio math is cleaner with simple returns.
167	  </p>
168	
169	  <div class="callout">
170	    <strong>The one identity that ties them together:</strong>
171	    <code>(1 + r<sup>simple</sup>) = exp(r<sup>log</sup>)</code>. For small returns
172	    (|r| &lt; 5%) they are numerically nearly identical. They diverge fast for
173	    crypto-sized moves; equities are usually fine.
174	  </div>
175	
176	  <!-- ===================== §2 VOL & SHARPE ===================== -->
177	  <h2>2. Volatility, the Sharpe ratio, and the annualization factor</h2>
178	
179	  <p>
180	    <strong>Volatility</strong> in finance just means "standard deviation of returns."
181	    By itself, that number is daily — small and unhelpful for comparison across
182	    horizons. So we always annualize:
183	  </p>
184	
185	  <div class="formula">
186	    σ<sub>ann</sub> = σ<sub>daily</sub> × √252
187	    <span class="small-note">252 ≈ number of US trading days per year</span>
188	  </div>
189	
190	  <h3>Why √252 and not 252?</h3>
191	  <p>
192	    If daily returns are independent with mean μ and variance σ², then over N days
193	    the sum has mean Nμ and variance Nσ². <strong>Means add; variances add.</strong>
194	    Annualized volatility is the standard deviation of the <em>annual</em> return,
195	    so you take the square root of the summed variance: √(252·σ²) = σ·√252. This
196	    is the central limit theorem doing its job.
197	  </p>
198	  <p class="small">
199	    The assumption of independence is wrong in detail (returns have autocorrelation,
200	    volatility clusters), but √T scaling is the universal first-pass convention and
